## Data Migration & Cutover Strategies

### Strategy Selection Matrix

| Strategy | Downtime | Risk | Data Volume | Schema Change | When to Use |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Big-bang dump/load | High (hours+) | Medium | < 100 GB | Any | Non-prod, batch systems, generous windows |
| Snapshot + replay | Medium | Medium | < 1 TB | Light | Same-engine cloud lift-and-shift |
| Managed migration service (DMS / ADF / Datastream) | Low | Low/Med | TB scale | Light to moderate | Most cloud moves |
| CDC replication (Debezium / GoldenGate / Striim) | Near-zero | Medium | Any | Moderate | Cross-engine, long migration windows |
| Dual-write strangler | Near-zero | High (complexity) | Any | Heavy | Engine swap with paradigm shift |
| Expand-contract | Zero | Low | Any | Heavy | Within-DB refactors before engine change |
| Read replica promotion | Minutes | Low | Any | None | Same-engine, when CDC replica supported |

### Recommended Default: Expand → Backfill → Dual-write → Shadow-read → Cutover → Contract

1. **Expand** target schema in parallel; deploy app changes that compile against new schema but still use old; add feature flag for read source / write destinations.
2. **Backfill** historical data via bulk load or CDC initial snapshot. Tools: AWS DMS, Azure DMS/ADF, GCP DMS/Datastream, GoldenGate, `pg_dump`/`mysqldump`/`bcp`/`expdp`, custom chunked ETL. Verify row counts + checksums.
3. **Dual-write** new transactions to both:
   - Synchronous in-app (XA/2PC if supported; compensate on failure)
   - Outbox (write source + outbox in one local tx; publisher streams to target)
   - CDC-based (only write source; CDC streams async)
   - Idempotency required; define LWW or version-based conflict policy.
4. **Shadow-read**: wrap read path; issue same query to source (primary) and target (shadow); return source; async-log mismatches. Sample 1–10% (high-volume) to 100% (low-volume/critical). Target < 0.01% mismatch.
5. **Cutover** — pre-checks: replication lag < threshold, mismatches resolved, backup taken, rollback dry-run done. Switch via feature flag or DNS / connection-string swap. Post-checks: errors flat, latency p99 within budget, DB CPU/IOPS sane.
6. **Contract** — disable writes on source (DENY/revoke); keep read-only for rollback window (7–30 days); decommission after sign-off.

### Rollback

Define abort criteria before cutover: error rate above baseline, latency above budget, data mismatch threshold, resource saturation. Dry-run procedure in staging. Typical: flip flag back; resume source; replay divergent writes from CDC stream; communicate.

### Performance Hardening Pre-Migration

- Drop **non-PK indexes** on target during bulk load; recreate after.
- Disable triggers during bulk load; re-enable.
- `UNLOGGED` (PG) or `BULK_LOGGED` (SQL Server) during load, then convert.
- `ANALYZE` / `UPDATE STATISTICS` immediately after load.
- Validate query plans on representative queries before opening traffic.

### Validation Pre-Cutover

- [ ] Row counts match per table (or expected delta documented)
- [ ] Checksum / hash of sampled rows match
- [ ] FK / unique / check constraints validated on target
- [ ] Sequences / identity aligned (target > source max + buffer)
- [ ] Indexes present and used (verify plans on top-N queries)
- [ ] Stats refreshed
- [ ] Connection pool sized for target
- [ ] Backup taken on source
- [ ] Rollback procedure dry-run done
- [ ] Monitoring dashboards live for target

### Migration Always-Flag

- Identity / sequence collisions during dual-write
- Timezone handling shifts in flight
- Encoding conversion errors (latin1 → utf8mb4)
- Triggers on target firing during bulk load
- FKs violated due to load order — load in topological order
- Soft-delete patterns differing between source and target

